00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:38,580
to get anywhere in the ballpark.

45
00:21:38,580 --> 00:21:42,700
And then how about conditioning insurance premium on sex?

46
00:21:42,700 --> 00:21:44,900
Is that OK?

47
00:21:44,900 --> 00:21:46,860
It depends a lot.

48
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:47,380
Right?

49
00:21:47,380 --> 00:21:50,420
And a cynic would say, well, if men are higher risk,

50
00:21:50,420 --> 00:21:51,900
then it's OK.

51
00:21:51,900 --> 00:21:52,400
Right?

52
00:21:52,400 --> 00:21:55,060
Like on auto insurance, men have higher risk.

53
00:21:55,060 --> 00:21:57,700
And I guess anyone here who's a male driver

54
00:21:57,700 --> 00:22:00,140
and you were charged what seemed like exorbitant rates when

55
00:22:00,140 --> 00:22:02,220
you were a teenager, or your parents were charged

56
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:03,500
the exorbitant rates.

57
00:22:03,500 --> 00:22:06,580
And then any decent economists say, yeah, well,

58
00:22:06,580 --> 00:22:08,980
males are on average really dangerous drivers.

59
00:22:08,980 --> 00:22:09,480
Right?

60
00:22:09,480 --> 00:22:11,660
And suppose that as a 16-year-old male driver,

61
00:22:11,660 --> 00:22:14,980
you would say, well, I've never been in an accident.

62
00:22:14,980 --> 00:22:18,580
It's not fair that I get charged more than a woman who's

63
00:22:18,580 --> 00:22:20,260
not been in an accident.

64
00:22:20,260 --> 00:22:22,500
And what is the honest statistical answer

65
00:22:22,500 --> 00:22:24,820
that a company probably doesn't want to tell you about?

66
00:22:24,820 --> 00:22:25,580
What's the truth?

67
00:22:25,580 --> 00:22:26,500
It's coming.

68
00:22:26,500 --> 00:22:27,000
It's coming.

69
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:28,900
It's coming.

70
00:22:28,900 --> 00:22:29,820
Probably.

71
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:32,700
Probably coming.

72
00:22:32,700 --> 00:22:36,500
There is always this individual level of justice of, look,

73
00:22:36,500 --> 00:22:39,380
you don't know it, but I happen to be an incredibly safe male

74
00:22:39,380 --> 00:22:39,880
driver.

75
00:22:39,880 --> 00:22:41,660
It's like, well, good for you.

76
00:22:41,660 --> 00:22:43,620
In 10 years, I'll believe you.

77
00:22:43,620 --> 00:22:47,100
But first, I'm going to, when essentially you

78
00:22:47,100 --> 00:22:49,020
have no track record, then you're

79
00:22:49,020 --> 00:22:51,780
going to base your view on general statistics.

80
00:22:51,780 --> 00:22:53,580
And then as your track record accumulates,

81
00:22:53,580 --> 00:22:56,100
you'll put more and more weight on the track record.

82
00:22:56,100 --> 00:22:58,460
Although, if it's for a very rare event,

83
00:22:58,460 --> 00:23:01,300
then you might continue to put a little weight on the track

84
00:23:01,300 --> 00:23:03,660
record, just because rare events usually don't happen,

85
00:23:03,660 --> 00:23:06,260
even for high-risk people.

86
00:23:06,260 --> 00:23:09,780
So if there's never been a nuclear accident

87
00:23:09,780 --> 00:23:12,740
in your country, they say, hey, we got a perfect success rating.

88
00:23:12,740 --> 00:23:15,460
So insurance should basically be free.

89
00:23:15,460 --> 00:23:18,460
Yeah, but you wouldn't expect Belgium

90
00:23:18,460 --> 00:23:20,980
to have had any accidents statistically.

91
00:23:20,980 --> 00:23:23,980
But nevertheless, the expected loss

92
00:23:23,980 --> 00:23:27,420
is high enough that it's going to be a high rate.

93
00:23:27,420 --> 00:23:28,780
So you've got all the regulations

94
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:31,060
that forbid insurers from conditioning premium

95
00:23:31,060 --> 00:23:33,260
on biocharacteristics, which again,

96
00:23:33,260 --> 00:23:37,020
is directly contradictory to the idea that companies are like,

97
00:23:37,020 --> 00:23:39,100
I don't know how risky anybody is.

98
00:23:39,100 --> 00:23:40,140
Poor me.

99
00:23:40,140 --> 00:23:42,380
Instead, this is the insurance companies

100
00:23:42,380 --> 00:23:44,180
do know how risky people are.

101
00:23:44,180 --> 00:23:45,540
And if we let them, they're going

102
00:23:45,540 --> 00:23:47,140
to charge high rates to risky people

103
00:23:47,140 --> 00:23:49,220
and low rates to safe people.

104
00:23:49,220 --> 00:23:51,980
And this is, of course, the very thing

105
00:23:51,980 --> 00:23:53,500
that you would do if you didn't want

106
00:23:53,500 --> 00:23:56,460
there to be adverse selection.

107
00:23:56,460 --> 00:23:59,180
Now, what does a normal person actually think?

108
00:23:59,180 --> 00:24:01,380
It's one thing to go and hear about adverse selection

109
00:24:01,380 --> 00:24:04,300
and then say, oh, that's why this stuff is good.

110
00:24:04,300 --> 00:24:07,180
But if you just were to go and talk to your grandmom

111
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:09,740
or something like that and say, isn't it terrible

112
00:24:09,740 --> 00:24:12,780
that you can't charge risky people high rates?

113
00:24:12,780 --> 00:24:15,140
What is a normal human reaction to an insurance company

114
00:24:15,140 --> 00:24:17,980
charging a risky person a high rate?

115
00:24:17,980 --> 00:24:18,700
It's not fair.

116
00:24:18,700 --> 00:24:19,380
You're mean.

117
00:24:19,380 --> 00:24:20,380
You're greedy.

118
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:23,180
It's like, well, why aren't you greedy for not offering

119
00:24:23,180 --> 00:24:25,380
to insure people to loss?

120
00:24:25,380 --> 00:24:27,660
Why is it that just because I call myself an insurance

121
00:24:27,660 --> 00:24:31,500
company, I'm expected to throw money to total strangers?

122
00:24:31,500 --> 00:24:33,060
But that comes with the territory.

123
00:24:33,060 --> 00:24:34,700
If you want to be an insurance company,

124
00:24:34,700 --> 00:24:37,340
you have to lose money on some bad risks.

125
00:24:37,340 --> 00:24:39,020
That's the expectation.

126
00:24:39,020 --> 00:24:43,020
Now, again, I will say that if your high-cost people are

127
00:24:43,020 --> 00:24:45,820
kids with leukemia, I at least understand

128
00:24:45,820 --> 00:24:47,260
the fairness intuition.

129
00:24:47,260 --> 00:24:51,540
If the high-risk people are bad drivers in New Jersey,

130
00:24:51,540 --> 00:24:53,140
this is where I just completely lose

131
00:24:53,140 --> 00:24:55,060
contact with what normal people are thinking.

132
00:24:55,060 --> 00:24:57,460
Like, what are you talking about?

133
00:24:57,460 --> 00:24:59,540
You want them to be driving?

134
00:24:59,540 --> 00:25:01,940
Wouldn't it be best if they had to pay really high rates

135
00:25:01,940 --> 00:25:05,020
and therefore stop driving?

136
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:08,340
And then this is where someone like my dad,

137
00:25:08,340 --> 00:25:10,940
who has a lifelong love affair with the automobile,

138
00:25:10,940 --> 00:25:14,620
will make an argument that makes a lot of sense to people,

139
00:25:14,620 --> 00:25:17,580
although it seems completely erasing me as a Brian.

140
00:25:17,580 --> 00:25:20,620
People have to drive.

141
00:25:20,620 --> 00:25:22,100
Don't you understand?

142
00:25:22,100 --> 00:25:24,380
People have to drive.

143
00:25:24,380 --> 00:25:26,500
And since they have to drive, it's

144
00:25:26,500 --> 00:25:30,740
not fair to go and charge that much money to Captain Death

145
00:25:30,740 --> 00:25:32,820
behind the wheel.

146
00:25:32,820 --> 00:25:35,540
Captain Death has to drive.

147
00:25:35,540 --> 00:25:37,780
Has he referred to Captain Death directly?

148
00:25:37,780 --> 00:25:39,660
Or is this just?

149
00:25:39,660 --> 00:25:40,940
I think I just made him up now.

150
00:25:40,940 --> 00:25:41,440
Yeah.

151
00:25:44,300 --> 00:25:45,940
Still, I will say this seems very odd.

152
00:25:45,940 --> 00:25:48,540
It's like, look, if he lost his license,

153
00:25:48,540 --> 00:25:52,020
then the I have to drive excuse wouldn't count.

154
00:25:52,020 --> 00:25:54,060
And you wouldn't mind jailing him for that

155
00:25:54,060 --> 00:25:56,180
if Captain Death kept driving around

156
00:25:56,180 --> 00:25:58,100
without a license after a vote.

157
00:25:58,100 --> 00:26:01,980
It's like, well, if you're sentenced in a court of law,

158
00:26:01,980 --> 00:26:05,860
then the I have to drive defense is overruled.

159
00:26:05,860 --> 00:26:08,180
But if it's just a matter of economic incentives

160
00:26:08,180 --> 00:26:10,900
that no one wants to insure you, then that's fine.

161
00:26:10,900 --> 00:26:13,580
And probably a better story would just be, well,

162
00:26:13,580 --> 00:26:15,540
if they don't go, if you charge them too much,

163
00:26:15,540 --> 00:26:16,780
they'll just drive without insurance.

164
00:26:16,780 --> 00:26:18,380
And we're worried about all the people

165
00:26:18,380 --> 00:26:20,140
that are victimized without the insurance.

166
00:26:20,140 --> 00:26:22,460
But again, you might say, well, how about you

167
00:26:22,460 --> 00:26:24,500
get 30 years in jail if you get in an accident

168
00:26:24,500 --> 00:26:26,140
after driving without insurance?

169
00:26:26,140 --> 00:26:29,380
We could do that instead of making insurance companies

170
00:26:29,380 --> 00:26:32,820
go and give heavily subsidized insurance to high-risk people,

171
00:26:32,820 --> 00:26:35,540
which causes which one of these two things?

172
00:26:35,540 --> 00:26:37,020
Does that?

173
00:26:37,020 --> 00:26:38,660
Actually, it's actually both.

174
00:26:38,660 --> 00:26:40,380
Yes, but the moral hazard as well.

175
00:26:40,380 --> 00:26:42,260
People that would have given up on driving

176
00:26:42,260 --> 00:26:46,220
because the rates are too high will then keep doing it.

177
00:26:46,220 --> 00:26:47,300
And then we'll see.

178
00:26:47,300 --> 00:26:49,720
I'm not sure, but I suspect that when you start getting up

179
00:26:49,720 --> 00:26:51,260
to very high ages, then there's also

180
00:26:51,260 --> 00:26:53,460
some regulation about what is the highest premium you

181
00:26:53,460 --> 00:26:56,020
can charge an 80-year-old.

182
00:26:56,020 --> 00:26:57,900
And again, on the one hand, we don't

183
00:26:57,900 --> 00:26:59,020
want 80-year-olds driving.

184
00:26:59,020 --> 00:27:01,140
On the other hand, we don't want them overcharged.

185
00:27:01,140 --> 00:27:05,620
So it's a tough situation for people

186
00:27:05,620 --> 00:27:10,780
with a clash of their populist intuitions.

187
00:27:10,780 --> 00:27:12,500
So anyway, there's regulations that

188
00:27:12,500 --> 00:27:14,200
forbid insurers from conditioning premium

189
00:27:14,200 --> 00:27:16,140
on buyer characteristics.

190
00:27:16,140 --> 00:27:18,940
There is subsidies for car insurance for reckless drivers

191
00:27:18,940 --> 00:27:20,860
or forcing insurers to cover their loss.

192
00:27:20,860 --> 00:27:22,580
Sometimes the state just pays a subsidy

193
00:27:22,580 --> 00:27:24,980
to go and make the insurance affordable for the reckless

194
00:27:24,980 --> 00:27:26,620
drivers.

195
00:27:26,620 --> 00:27:28,380
Reckless is such a harsh word.

196
00:27:28,380 --> 00:27:32,220
Let's just say multiple accident drivers,

197
00:27:32,220 --> 00:27:35,860
habitual accident-prone drivers.

198
00:27:35,860 --> 00:27:37,060
Doesn't mean you're reckless.

199
00:27:37,060 --> 00:27:38,940
Maybe you're just a bad driver.

200
00:27:38,940 --> 00:27:40,620
You're being very cautious, but you just

201
00:27:40,620 --> 00:27:43,420
keep ramming into people.

202
00:27:43,420 --> 00:27:47,100
And then, of course, there's the ban on free musician clauses.

203
00:27:47,100 --> 00:27:49,380
This has been properly ridiculed, especially

204
00:27:49,380 --> 00:27:50,700
for life insurance.

205
00:27:50,700 --> 00:27:52,460
It's like, well, why is it you have

206
00:27:52,460 --> 00:27:54,420
to buy life insurance while you're still alive?

207
00:27:54,420 --> 00:27:57,100
Why can't the dead buy it?

208
00:27:57,100 --> 00:27:59,820
And of course, if presumably your heirs

209
00:27:59,820 --> 00:28:03,660
would go and buy life insurance for you after your death,

210
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:05,700
what would be an obvious thing to do as soon

211
00:28:05,700 --> 00:28:09,140
as your beloved relatives pass away?

212
00:28:09,140 --> 00:28:11,020
Or head right down to the insurance companies

213
00:28:11,020 --> 00:28:14,820
and say, I would like to have a billion dollars of life

214
00:28:14,820 --> 00:28:16,180
insurance.

215
00:28:16,180 --> 00:28:18,460
And of course, if this were really going on,

216
00:28:18,460 --> 00:28:21,980
then remember when we were talking about how no company

217
00:28:21,980 --> 00:28:24,180
really will be able to sell insurance at an actually fair

218
00:28:24,180 --> 00:28:25,820
rate because they'd be losing money,

219
00:28:25,820 --> 00:28:27,380
because they're investing over overhead.

220
00:28:27,380 --> 00:28:29,540
So then the rates would be even more than a billion

221
00:28:29,540 --> 00:28:32,380
dollar policy, and it would destroy the markets.

222
00:28:32,380 --> 00:28:34,220
So that's a particularly fair case.

223
00:28:34,220 --> 00:28:37,700
But any of these cases do basically follow.

224
00:28:41,060 --> 00:28:45,380
So questions about the real world of regulation.

225
00:28:45,380 --> 00:28:48,740
So much of it which does seem to be specifically concerned

226
00:28:48,740 --> 00:28:51,620
with amplifying the two problems that economists usually

227
00:28:51,620 --> 00:28:54,860
use to justify the problem.

228
00:28:54,860 --> 00:28:56,780
Now, by the way, it is possible, of course,

229
00:28:56,780 --> 00:28:59,060
that insurance companies can have a lot of information,

230
00:28:59,060 --> 00:29:01,260
but there's still some residual after selection.

231
00:29:01,260 --> 00:29:03,300
Like it could be that insurance companies

232
00:29:03,300 --> 00:29:05,740
can make a very good prediction of your risk

233
00:29:05,740 --> 00:29:10,060
conditional upon your age, sex, occupation, location,

234
00:29:10,060 --> 00:29:12,220
a bunch of other factors.

235
00:29:12,220 --> 00:29:15,700
Health history, of course, is another really good one to use.

236
00:29:15,700 --> 00:29:18,260
And yet, you could still have a little bit

237
00:29:18,260 --> 00:29:22,100
of additional information on top of all the information

238
00:29:22,100 --> 00:29:24,020
in the database of the insurance company.

239
00:29:24,020 --> 00:29:25,620
That's going to be a marginal problem,

240
00:29:25,620 --> 00:29:27,620
but there could still be a little bit of problem

241
00:29:27,620 --> 00:29:28,940
left on top.

242
00:29:28,940 --> 00:29:34,780
But again, do not underestimate the amazing power of actuaries

243
00:29:34,780 --> 00:29:37,420
and how much they know.

244
00:29:37,420 --> 00:29:39,140
By the way, actuary is a great profession

245
00:29:39,140 --> 00:29:42,060
if you like taking tests.

246
00:29:42,060 --> 00:29:43,580
So my best friend at Princeton did

247
00:29:43,580 --> 00:29:45,020
economic consulting for two years

248
00:29:45,020 --> 00:29:46,380
and said this is terrible.

249
00:29:46,380 --> 00:29:48,260
I hate every second of this.

250
00:29:48,260 --> 00:29:51,060
And then he was the math guru in our program.

251
00:29:51,060 --> 00:29:53,460
So he was the one who we would always go to with our math.

252
00:29:53,460 --> 00:29:56,700
And so he was able to go and pass the first actuarial test

253
00:29:56,700 --> 00:29:57,900
with almost no work.

254
00:29:57,900 --> 00:30:00,020
And then he just became an actuary.

255
00:30:00,020 --> 00:30:03,420
And then the tests were so easy for him.

256
00:30:03,420 --> 00:30:06,060
And the way that it works is there's seven tests

257
00:30:06,060 --> 00:30:07,620
to go and complete the program.

258
00:30:07,620 --> 00:30:09,380
And for each test, a company will generally

259
00:30:09,380 --> 00:30:10,860
give you two or three weeks vacation

260
00:30:10,860 --> 00:30:12,500
to study for the test.

261
00:30:12,500 --> 00:30:14,540
Since my friend didn't actually need to study,

262
00:30:14,540 --> 00:30:18,940
he just got six extra free paid vacations.

263
00:30:18,940 --> 00:30:21,780
So he would fly to Europe for a couple weeks

264
00:30:21,780 --> 00:30:24,300
and then come back and be like, no, I'm ready for this test.

265
00:30:24,300 --> 00:30:27,020
It's going to be real hard.

266
00:30:27,020 --> 00:30:29,700
Most people, in fact, few people on Earth

267
00:30:29,700 --> 00:30:34,500
would be at his level, which is why he was helping all of us.

268
00:30:34,500 --> 00:30:36,080
And he's a great job if you want to have

269
00:30:36,080 --> 00:30:39,500
an upper middle class income and only work 35 hours a week

270
00:30:39,500 --> 00:30:40,700
for the rest of your life.

271
00:30:40,700 --> 00:30:41,500
Retire at 60.

272
00:30:44,220 --> 00:30:45,660
He's never considered it exciting.

273
00:30:45,660 --> 00:30:48,100
But it does leave an enormous amount of time

274
00:30:48,100 --> 00:30:50,900
for his many exciting hobbies.

275
00:30:50,900 --> 00:30:53,540
This point, well, it was a good backup plan, anyway,

276
00:30:53,540 --> 00:30:56,700
for undergraduates.

277
00:30:56,700 --> 00:30:58,100
He finished his PhD at Princeton.

278
00:30:58,100 --> 00:31:02,820
He's like, wow, that didn't do me any good at all.

279
00:31:02,820 --> 00:31:03,460
OK.

280
00:31:03,460 --> 00:31:05,260
Now, there has been some empirical work

281
00:31:05,260 --> 00:31:07,220
on the severity of adverse selection.

282
00:31:07,220 --> 00:31:10,180
And here's this frightening thing.

283
00:31:10,180 --> 00:31:12,580
At the time that this was being invoked

284
00:31:12,580 --> 00:31:17,220
as a ironclad argument in favor of Obamacare,

285
00:31:17,220 --> 00:31:19,060
there were already some published papers

286
00:31:19,060 --> 00:31:20,860
saying that adverse selection doesn't even

287
00:31:20,860 --> 00:31:22,700
seem to be much of a problem.

288
00:31:22,700 --> 00:31:25,260
It's not even clear that it exists.

289
00:31:25,260 --> 00:31:28,020
Or maybe there is reverse adverse selection,

290
00:31:28,020 --> 00:31:29,100
as we'll see.

291
00:31:29,100 --> 00:31:29,980
All right.

292
00:31:29,980 --> 00:31:32,460
So anyway, so two takes on why there's

293
00:31:32,460 --> 00:31:34,580
so little evidence for adverse selection,

294
00:31:34,580 --> 00:31:38,020
except in cases where regulation causes adverse selection.

295
00:31:38,020 --> 00:31:39,660
So there are studies of cases where

296
00:31:39,660 --> 00:31:41,900
you have extreme regulations, which

297
00:31:41,900 --> 00:31:43,380
do cause adverse selection.

298
00:31:43,380 --> 00:31:45,380
But that's, of course, not the kind of evidence

299
00:31:45,380 --> 00:31:48,180
that some advocate of a market failure theory is looking for.

300
00:31:48,180 --> 00:31:50,220
You want to find an unregulated market where

301
00:31:50,220 --> 00:31:52,260
the market has been destroyed.

302
00:31:52,260 --> 00:31:55,780
Anyway, so two different takes.

303
00:31:55,780 --> 00:31:58,020
So one of them is that insurance companies actually

304
00:31:58,020 --> 00:32:00,340
know more about you than you do.

305
00:32:00,340 --> 00:32:03,380
So if an actuary disagrees with your self-assessment

306
00:32:03,380 --> 00:32:05,100
of your own health or your own risk,

307
00:32:05,100 --> 00:32:07,220
maybe you should just leave the actuary,

308
00:32:07,220 --> 00:32:08,900
throw away what you think you know.

309
00:32:08,900 --> 00:32:10,940
So they have these actuary tables, and you don't.

