170
01:06:51,360 --> 01:06:58,360
All right, now, I mention all of these puzzles because they all have received very similar solutions.

171
01:06:58,360 --> 01:07:02,360
And a similar solution goes under the heading of signaling.

172
01:07:02,360 --> 01:07:05,360
All right, so the basic assumption of all these models.

173
01:07:05,360 --> 01:07:11,360
First of all, you need the assumption that there are different types of people and firms.

174
01:07:11,360 --> 01:07:16,360
All right, so you could have able and undisciplined workers, hard and dumb workers, honest and dishonest firms.

175
01:07:16,360 --> 01:07:17,360
Or working ladies.

176
01:07:17,360 --> 01:07:19,360
All kinds of possibilities.

177
01:07:19,360 --> 01:07:23,360
You could have psychotic and sane.

178
01:07:23,360 --> 01:07:25,360
All right, so things like that.

179
01:07:25,360 --> 01:07:28,360
All right, then, so that's the first assumption.

180
01:07:28,360 --> 01:07:31,360
Then it needs to be hard to observe the types directly.

181
01:07:31,360 --> 01:07:36,360
In other words, we need asymmetric information.

182
01:07:36,360 --> 01:07:40,360
So individuals know their type better than outsiders.

183
01:07:40,360 --> 01:07:45,360
You know what kind of worker you are better than employers know what you are.

184
01:07:45,360 --> 01:07:48,360
You know whether you are really a psychopath or not.

185
01:07:48,360 --> 01:07:50,360
At least you know better than others.

186
01:07:50,360 --> 01:07:55,360
There could still be some confusion about yourself, but you've got to know better where you stand than others do.

187
01:07:55,360 --> 01:08:04,360
All right, and then the third assumption, this is the key, is that different types have different costs of performing the same observable activity.

188
01:08:04,360 --> 01:08:09,360
All right, so we need to have different costs.

189
01:08:09,360 --> 01:08:12,360
And in particular, we need to have the type that is in higher demand.

190
01:08:12,360 --> 01:08:19,360
The type that is more desired by the market needs to have a lower cost of doing something that is observable.

191
01:08:19,360 --> 01:08:25,360
All right, and then we put this all together, and you say, hey, all right, well, suppose I'm the good type.

192
01:08:25,360 --> 01:08:29,360
But there's asymmetric info, so people don't know I'm the good type.

193
01:08:29,360 --> 01:08:30,360
So what am I going to do?

194
01:08:30,360 --> 01:08:32,360
I'm going to go and signal.

195
01:08:32,360 --> 01:08:36,360
I'm going to do some things that I wouldn't want to do unless I were the good type.

196
01:08:36,360 --> 01:08:42,360
And by doing this, I will convince observers of the goodness of my type.

197
01:08:42,360 --> 01:08:46,360
And, of course, this doesn't have to be perfect.

198
01:08:46,360 --> 01:08:49,360
It could be that you just raise the probability, right?

199
01:08:49,360 --> 01:08:58,360
But if you can go and move someone's probability that you're a good worker from 40% to 55%, that could be enough to get you over the top and get you hired.

200
01:08:58,360 --> 01:09:04,360
So it could be that some bad people are capable of sending the signal or will send it.

201
01:09:04,360 --> 01:09:08,360
But as long as it raises the probability, then it could easily be worthwhile.

202
01:09:08,360 --> 01:09:16,360
So what is a standard, really easy thing for someone to show that they are a textbook psychopath?

203
01:09:16,360 --> 01:09:26,360
So someone smoking a cigarette, what can they do to immediately convince you that they are, in fact, not to be trifled with?

204
01:09:26,360 --> 01:09:29,360
Put it out on themselves.

205
01:09:29,360 --> 01:09:31,360
All right?

206
01:09:31,360 --> 01:09:34,360
This is a very scary action.

207
01:09:34,360 --> 01:09:38,360
Look, I'm not talking about putting a cigarette out on you. That's scarier.

208
01:09:38,360 --> 01:09:41,360
But that, of course, immediately gets you in big trouble.

209
01:09:41,360 --> 01:09:44,360
You could go to jail for putting a cigarette out on another person. That's a good thing, too.

210
01:09:44,360 --> 01:09:51,360
But if you put a cigarette out on yourself, like a bunch of other people see this, people will talk.

211
01:09:51,360 --> 01:09:53,360
And people will not mess with the person.

212
01:09:53,360 --> 01:09:58,360
If he'll do that to himself, what would he do to another person?

213
01:09:58,360 --> 01:10:02,360
You don't want to mess with that person, steer clear of them.

214
01:10:02,360 --> 01:10:10,360
And of course this means that if you're ever in prison and you're not a psychopath, you might want to do this just so that the worst things don't happen.

215
01:10:10,360 --> 01:10:12,360
You might say, hey, look at me.

216
01:10:12,360 --> 01:10:13,360
All right?

217
01:10:13,360 --> 01:10:15,360
That is, of course, just one example.

218
01:10:15,360 --> 01:10:21,360
But for all of those different cases I gave you, we can think of examples.

219
01:10:21,360 --> 01:10:22,360
All right.

